# SCHEDULING PROTOCOL: placing recommended tasks into one person's calendar.

## INPUT WINDOW
schedule_start_date : {{ week_start }}
schedule_end_date   : {{ week_end }}
waking_window       : {{ wake_start }}–{{ sleep_start }}  # no scheduling outside this window

## DEFAULT PATH (read this first)

{% block default_path %}
For each task, ACTIVELY try a CONCURRENT placement when the task is conceptually
compatible with a busy event and can run in parallel with it; otherwise place it
STANDALONE in a free gap. Pick the BETTER of standalone vs a proper concurrent fit,
not standalone by default. Every persona has at least 50% of the day free, so a
STANDALONE gap is always there as the fallback.

CONCURRENT placement is ENCOURAGED, but use it ONLY when the "task" is a 
PROPER CONCURRENT FIT with user's planned "event" OR a "context" i.e. the two activities 
can genuinely be performed AT THE SAME TIME or because they are COMPLEMENTARY or is 
CONTEXTUALLY FIT.

When a day already has a busy event that is a PROPER CONCURRENT FIT for a task, prefer
placing the task CONCURRENT inside that event over opening a separate free gap; scan the
day's busy events for such a fit FIRST, then fall back to STANDALONE when none fits. For a
`dividable_ok: true` task, still prefer SPLITTING it over one concurrent block.
{% endblock %}

{% block fit_examples %}
Examples of PROPER concurrent fits:
  • task::"mindful eating" AND event::"Lunch": the person eats and pays attention to 
    the food AT THE SAME TIME (i.e., not watching movies while eating)
  • (task::"Doing 5 Squats", task::"Hydration Sip", or task::"Looking Outside") AND 
    event::"Office Work": micro-breaks DURING or AT THE SAME TIME as prolonged seated 
    activity.
  • (task::"Breathing Exercise" or task::Meditation) AND context::stress::anxiety:
    CONTEXTUALLY FIT
  • task::"Plan Sitting Breaks" AND context::behaviour_state::action_planning:
    CONTEXTUALLY FIT
  • (task::"Drink a Bottle of Water" or task::"Short Rest Break") AND
    (event::"Meeting" or event::"Office Work"): a sip or a brief pause DURING seated
    work, AT THE SAME TIME.
  • (task::"Breathing Exercise" or task::"Calm Stretch") AND
    (event::"Reading" or event::"Journaling"): a calm micro-activity DURING a calm
    seated slot.


Examples that are NOT proper fits:
  • task::"Light Stretching" AND event::"Grocery Shopping"; the body is doing the chore
  • task::"Lower-Sugar Drink" AND context::ttm_process::self_liberation; the proper context
    for such task is counter_conditioning not self_liberation (domain context links
    and definitions are given for recommended tasks)
  • Any task AND (event::Gaming or event::Gardening); hands and attention are occupied
  • Any intensive task (e.g., Running, Cycling, Cardio) AND event::eating (e.g, Lunch,
    Dinner): you cannot eat a meal while doing such intensive activities.
  • (task::"Meal Preparation" OR task::"Cooking") AND event::eating (e.g, Lunch,
    Dinner): preparation is sequential i.e., happens BEFORE the meal, not during it.
{% endblock %}

{% block rule_of_thumb_1 %}
RULE OF THUMB 1: "during X", "while X", "with X" in a task title or description is a
HINT, not a rule. Apply the proper-fit question above and ASK YOURSELF Can/should a 
human being who is in (context::X or event::Y) do (task::Z)? When the answer to this
question is "NO", choose STANDALONE in a free gap on the same day.
{% endblock %}

{% block rule_of_thumb_2 %}
RULE OF THUMB 2: a task title meaning Preparation (e.g., "prepare X", "before X", 
"ready for X", "for X") describes work that happens BEFORE the user's event, not during
it.  Place it STANDALONE in the free gap IMMEDIATELY PRECEDING the relevant user's event.

Examples:
  • task::"Prepare a Salad" is STANDALONE in the free gap before event::eating
    (e.g, Lunch, Dinner).
  • task::"Light Dinner Preparation" is STANDALONE in the free gap before event::Dinner 
    (e.g. 18:30–18:50 if Dinner starts 19:20).
  • task::"Get Ready for Bed" is STANDALONE before event::Sleep.
{% endblock %}

{% block rule_of_thumb_3 %}
RULE OF THUMB 3: when a task title literally CONTAINS the name of a user event OR 
describes the SAME activity with only minor changes in intensity, duration, count, 
style, or pace, the task should be placed CONCURRENT with the user's event, i.e.,
the task says HOW to do the activity that the user already scheduled, so they 
happen simultaneously by being the same activity. Containment math still applies i.e.,
the task's `[start, end]` must sit inside the user's interval.

Examples:
  • task::"Eat Mindfully" AND event::Lunch are CONCURRENT: same activity (eating); the
    task says HOW to eat during the meal.
  • task::"Read Something Calm" AND event::Reading are CONCURRENT: same reading activity
    with a specified focus, inside the existing slot.
  • (task::"Write One Difficult Thought" or task::"Write Three Gratitudes") AND 
    event::Journaling are CONCURRENT: Same reflective-writing with a specified topic
    (particular thoughts).
  • task::"Plan This Week's Meals" AND event::Journaling are CONCURRENT: Same sedentary
    writing activity with a specified topic.

Counter-cases: the event and task title share words but the activities are NOT the same:
  • task::"Plan Tomorrow's Lunch" AND event::Lunch are NOT concurrent: The task is
    planning, the user event is eating the food.
  • task::"Walk With Family" AND event::Visit Family are NOT CONCURRENT: The task is a
    separate walk; should be STANDALONE on a free gap.
When uncertain, choose STANDALONE.
{% endblock %}

{% block split_dividable_tasks %}
DIVIDE-FRIENDLY TASKS: when a task carries `dividable_ok: true`, prefer to SPLIT it 
into TWO OR MORE shorter easier sibling sessions across the week instead of placing it
as one long block. Schedule these as a `pieces` array inside that task's output entry.

INSTRUCTIONS to SPLIT:
  • `dividable_ok == true` is the only gate. Tasks without this flag MUST stay as ONE
    placement (use the top-level `date` / `start` / `end` fields);
  • Splitting is most useful for longer tasks (e.g. `duration_max >= 30`), where two or
    three short sessions feel more feasible than one long block.
  • Place the pieces on DIFFERENT DAYS, or at least in DIFFERENT free gaps on the same
    day; never back-to-back inside one gap.

PER-PIECE CONSTRAINTS (each piece is its own placement)
  • `duration_min ≤ (piece.end − piece.start) < duration_max`.
    STRICT upper bound: every piece must be SHORTER than the
    original `duration_max`; a piece equal to `duration_max` is
    not credited as a divide.
  • Each piece must satisfy the WAKING-WINDOW INEQUALITIES
    (`{{ wake_start }} ≤ piece.start` and `piece.end ≤
    {{ sleep_start }}`).
  • Each piece may be STANDALONE in a free gap or CONCURRENT with a user's event, 
    independently of the other pieces.

WHOLE-SPLIT TARGET
  • The pieces are sessions of ONE weekly habit, so TOGETHER they should add up to about
    its FULL `duration_max`, not just `duration_min`. Aim the total near `duration_max`.
  • Three 20-minute pieces sum to 60 ≈ `duration_max=60`; two 35-minute pieces sum
    to 70 ≈ `duration_max=60`. Two 10-minute pieces (sum 20) fall far short; two 50-minute
    pieces (sum 100) run too long.

OUTPUT FORMAT FOR A SPLIT
  Use the `pieces` array INSTEAD of the top-level
  `date` / `start` / `end` / `concurrent_with_event` fields:

    {
      "task_index": 7,
      "task_title": "Walk 6,000 Steps",
      "scheduled": true,
      "pieces": [
        {"date": "2026-05-04", "start": "07:30", "end": "07:50",
         "concurrent_with_event": null},
        {"date": "2026-05-06", "start": "18:00", "end": "18:20",
         "concurrent_with_event": null},
        {"date": "2026-05-08", "start": "12:30", "end": "12:50",
         "concurrent_with_event": null}
      ]
    }

  Per-piece `concurrent_with_event` follows the standard rule
  (user's event label VERBATIM or null). Splitting is OPTIONAL; if a
  single placement is more natural for a `dividable_ok: true`
  task, the standard top-level form is still valid.
{% endblock %}

## STEP 1: DAY TIMELINES (already built for you, USE AS GROUND TRUTH)

Each day below is a strictly ordered JSON array of intervals.
Interval types:
  - "busy" : an existing calendar event with `label`, `start`, `end`.
             Apply the proper-fit test from above to decide whether this event can host
             a task concurrently.
  - "free" : open time. Use these for STANDALONE placement whenever the gap's 
             `duration_min` ≥ the task's `duration_min`.
  - "done" : a task already placed in an earlier week (slot is taken).

Use the interval's `label` field VERBATIM whenever you reference it in your
response.

