## STEP 3: RULES for PLACEMENT of RECOMMENDED TASKS  

For EACH recommended task choose ONE placement approach: 

(1) STANDALONE placement:
  Walk the day's timeline and pick the FIRST `free` interval whose
  `duration_min` ≥ the task's `duration_min`. Inside that gap, choose
  `[start, end]` with `end − start` between `duration_min` and
  `duration_max`. Set "concurrent_with_event": null.

{% block worked_example_standalone %}
    Worked example: task::"Do Light Stretching", duration_min=10, duration_max=20.
    Day timeline includes: {"type":"free","start":"07:07","end":"08:40","duration_min":93}:
    93 ≥ 10 and the gap is wide enough -> PLACE STANDALONE
    start="07:30", end="07:45" (15 min, comfortably inside the gap).
{% endblock %}

(2) CONCURRENT placement
  All conditions must hold STRICTLY:
    1. recommended task `concurrent_ok` == true
    2. PROPER CONCURRENT FIT: the task and the user's event and context match
       (based on DEFAULT PATH instructions)
    3. CONTAINMENT (read carefully; most rejections come from this):
         (i)   busy.start ≤ task.start
         (ii)  task.end ≤ busy.end
         (iii) task.start < task.end
         (iv)  duration_min ≤ (task.end − task.start) ≤ duration_max
       The task's end-time MUST NOT exceed the user's event end-time.

{% block worked_example_concurrent %}
    Worked example: user's event::"Reading" planned 20:00–20:30 (a 30-minute event),
    task::"Reading something calm" duration band is 10–20 minutes.
        VALID:  20:00–20:20 or 20:05–20:25 or 20:10–20:30 or 20:00–20:30
        INVALID: 20:30–20:50 (starts at user's event end, runs past it)
        INVALID: 20:25–20:45 (ends 15 min past user's event end)
        INVALID: 19:55–20:15 (starts 5 min before user's event)
{% endblock %}

  Set "concurrent_with_event" to the busy interval's `label` value VERBATIM.

{% block hard_constraints %}
HARD CONSTRAINTS (any violation mandates choosing a different slot):
  - duration_min ≤ (end − start) ≤ duration_max (in MINUTES)
  - WAKING-WINDOW INEQUALITIES (BOTH must hold):
       {{ wake_start }} ≤ task.start
       task.end ≤ {{ sleep_start }}
{% block worked_example_waking %}
    Worked example: waking_window {{ wake_start }}–{{ sleep_start }}:
        VALID:   06:30–06:45 or 21:30–21:55 or {{ wake_start }}–06:30 or
                 21:40–{{ sleep_start }}
        INVALID: 04:22–04:32 (starts before wake)
        INVALID: 21:55–22:05 (ends 5 min past sleep)
        INVALID: 22:00–22:10 (starts at sleep boundary)
        INVALID: 05:55–06:10 (starts before wake)
{% endblock %}
  - Two tasks you place may NOT overlap each other on the SAME DAY, EXCEPT when each 
    is concurrent with a DIFFERENT busy interval.
  - Each busy interval HOSTS AT MOST ONE concurrent task in this response.
  - At most one task with intensity ≥ 4 per calendar date.
{% endblock %}

{% block do_not_drop %}
SCHEDULE EVERY TASK. Leave NO task unscheduled.
  Every persona has enough free time, so every task fits somewhere this week.
  If no obvious slot exists, choose ANY free gap on ANY day that satisfies the
  duration band, even if it is not a "perfect" fit. Setting `scheduled: false`
  drops the task and is almost never justified.
  The ONLY acceptable reason is: every free interval on every day in the week is
  shorter than task::`duration_min` AND no busy interval is a proper-fit host.
{% endblock %}

{% block self_verify %}
SELF-VERIFY (do this silently inside your reasoning, NOT in the output) BEFORE finalizing 
  the JSON array, walk through every entry where `scheduled` is true and re-check ALL
  numeric constraints in your head:
    • waking-window inequalities ({{ wake_start }} ≤ task.start AND task.end ≤ 
      {{ sleep_start }})
    • duration band (duration_min ≤ end − start ≤ duration_max)
    • when concurrent: STRICT user's event containment (event.start ≤ task.start AND task.end 
      ≤ event.end)
    • when `context_recommends` is non-empty for the task: at least one recommended-category
      episode in the chosen day's CONTEXT block must overlap `[task.start, task.end]`;
      if no episode of those categories exists on the chosen day, the requirement is
      vacuously satisfied (there is nothing to align with). Hard rules above still win
      when they conflict.
  If any check fails, FIX that entry BEFORE writing it out. This self-check is part of
  the same response, do NOT mention it, the reasoning, or the corrections in the output.
{% endblock %}

## STEP 4: OUTPUT (STRICT)

Return ONLY a valid JSON array. Exactly {{ num_tasks }} objects, one per `task_index`,
in any order. No markdown fences, no commentary, no chain-of-thought.

A task entry takes ONE of three shapes: single placement, split into pieces (only when
`dividable_ok: true`), or unscheduled.

[
  {
    "task_index": 1,
    "task_title": "<copy of task_title>",
    "scheduled": true,
    "date": "YYYY-MM-DD",
    "start": "HH:MM",
    "end": "HH:MM",
    "concurrent_with_event": "<busy label or null>"
  },
  {
    "task_index": 2,
    "task_title": "<copy of task_title>",
    "scheduled": true,
    "pieces": [
      {"date": "YYYY-MM-DD", "start": "HH:MM", "end": "HH:MM",
       "concurrent_with_event": "<busy label or null>"},
      {"date": "YYYY-MM-DD", "start": "HH:MM", "end": "HH:MM",
       "concurrent_with_event": "<busy label or null>"}
    ]
  },
  {
    "task_index": 3,
    "task_title": "...",
    "scheduled": false
  }
]